acking china  had signed up  to belton road efforts to shape global  perceptions come  right from the very top china's  president xi jinping was the opening  speaker at this year's virtual  davos meeting exuding confidence and  positioning beijing  as the world's greatest champion of  global cooperation  there is a strong desire to show the  superiority of you know the chinese  system  to set to give  examples that show why perhaps in the  20th century democracies were  cute and useful and good but now for the  21st century the only systems that are  really suitable to face the challenges  of this century  are systems that are closer to the  chinese system  us president joe biden has made his  debut on the world stage vowing to push  back at that idea  saying democracy will and must prevail  germany's angela merkel says that the  west must now engage in this unfolding  competition  it's important that it's not only china  and russia that are delivering vaccines  to developing countries  the unfixings it might seem like madness  that lining up for a vaccine can mean  picking sides in a geopolitical battle  but that is shaping up to be our new  reality  a battle for influence and even ideas  about nothing less than how to run the  world  a report by dw's chief international  editor richard walker who  joins us now richard we heard in your  report that beijing is promoting a  counter-narrative about the origins of  the pandemic trying to convince people  it didn't start in china  is it gaining any traction with that  effort  well terry it's really too early to say  in this phase where we're talking about  vaccines diplomacy at the moment  obviously this has really only just  begun but  the latest research that we do have from  just towards the end of last year from  pew research just showed that especially  in western countries or countries  that are members of the kind of  democratic community of the west  they have a massive image problem so  negative evaluations of 